inflection after 2019, peaking at 10.5m.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Europe & North America comparison\",\n        \"tips\": \"Contrast Europe's steady rise (to 4.8m) with North America's slower growth (to 2.6m).\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The line graph delineates the annual volume of battery electric vehicle sales across China, Europe, and North America over a ten-year period from 2015 to 2025, measured in millions of units.\\n\\nOverall, it is immediately apparent that all three regions experienced substantial upward trajectories in electric vehicle adoption over the period. However, China demonstrated the most dramatic expansion, outstripping its Western counterparts by a considerable margin from 2019 onwards.\\n\\nIn 2015, sales across all three jurisdictions remained modest, with China recording approximately 0.2 million units, followed closely by Europe at 0.15 million and North America at 0.1 million. While growth remained moderate through 2019, China's market accelerated exponentially thereafter, leaping from 1.2 million in 2019 to 3.3 million in 2021, before climbing steeply to culminate at 10.5 million sales by 2025.\\n\\nIn contrast, Europe and North America followed steadier trajectories. European sales rose progressively to reach 2.3 million in 2021 and peaked at 4.8 million in 2025. North America registered the slowest adoption rate throughout the timeframe; nevertheless, its sales grew consistently from 0.4 million in 2019 to finish at 2.6 million at the end of the survey period.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Task Achievement: Covers all key trends with precise data comparisons. Cohesion: Logical sequencing with seamless transitions ('thereafter', 'in contrast'). Lexical Resource: Rich, precise vocabulary ('upward trajectories', 'outstripping', 'modest', 'culminate at'). Grammar: Flawless complex sentence structures.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Included clear overview paragraph\",\n      \"No personal opinions or outside facts\",\n      \"Exact figures cited with units (millions)\",\n      \"Key comparisons made between regions\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"acad-t1-02\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task1_academic\",\n    \"title\": \"Renewable Energy Share by Country (2024)\",\n    \"chartType\": \"bar\",\n    \"promptText\": \"The bar chart below compares the percentage share of national electricity generated by three renewable sources—Solar, Wind, and Hydroelectric—in five countries in 2024.\\n\\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 150,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 20,\n    \"chartData\": [\n      {\n        \"country\": \"Germany\",\n        \"Solar\": 18,\n        \"Wind\": 32,\n        \"Hydro\": 4\n      },\n      {\n        \"country\": \"UK\",\n        \"Solar\": 8,\n        \"Wind\": 29,\n        \"Hydro\": 3\n      },\n      {\n        \"country\": \"Australia\",\n        \"Solar\": 22,\n        \"Wind\": 14,\n        \"Hydro\": 6\n      },\n      {\n        \"country\": \"Brazil\",\n        \"Solar\": 9,\n        \"Wind\": 13,\n        \"Hydro\": 62\n      },\n      {\n        \"country\": \"Canada\",\n        \"Solar\": 2,\n        \"Wind\": 7,\n        \"Hydro\": 58\n      }\n    ],\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase the prompt\",\n        \"tips\": \"Identify countries, energy modalities, year (2024), and percentage metric.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Overview\",\n        \"focus\": \"Dominant sources per nation\",\n        \"tips\": \"Note that Hydro dominated Brazil and Canada, while Wind and Solar dominated Germany and Australia.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 1\",\n        \"focus\": \"Hydroelectric powerhouses (Brazil & Canada)\",\n        \"tips\": \"Report Brazil (62%) and Canada (58%), with minimal solar/wind contributions.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Wind and Solar leaders (Germany, UK, Australia)\",\n        \"tips\": \"Report Germany's leading wind share (32%), Australia's solar peak (22%), and UK's wind preference.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The bar chart illustrates the proportion of domestic electricity generated from solar, wind, and hydroelectric power across five nations—Germany, the United Kingdom, Australia, Brazil, and Canada—in the year 2024.\\n\\nOverall, the data reveals stark geographical polarities in clean energy portfolios. While Brazil and Canada relied predominantly on hydroelectric power, Germany, the UK, and Australia derived the bulk of their renewable output from wind and solar generation.\\n\\nHydroelectricity formed the cornerstone of power generation in the Americas. Brazil led all surveyed nations with hydroelectricity accounting for an impressive 62% of its grid, while Canada followed closely at 58%. In sharp contrast, neither country generated significant shares from solar or wind, with Canada recording negligible figures of 2% and 7% respectively.\\n\\nConversely, European nations and Australia prioritized wind and solar infrastructure. Germany generated 32% of its power from wind and 18% from solar, maintaining just 4% from hydro. Similarly, the UK relied heavily on wind at 29%, though its solar share stood at only 8%. Australia was distinguished by having the highest solar contribution among all five countries at 22%, accompanied by 14% from wind and 6% from hydro.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Clear categorization of countries by profile. Accurate data extraction with effective contrasting markers ('In sharp contrast', 'Conversely'). Solid band 8 lexical accuracy.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\":