  {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Contracting categories (Food, Transport, Leisure)\",\n        \"tips\": \"Detail drops in Food (24% to 20%), Transport (18% to 14%), and Leisure (17% to 12%).\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The two pie charts compare the proportional distribution of weekly household expenditure across five key sectors in Australia between 2010 and 2020.\\n\\nOverall, essential living expenses—specifically housing and utilities—absorbed a noticeably larger portion of Australian family budgets over the decade, whereas proportions dedicated to food, transport, and discretionary leisure experienced noticeable contractions.\\n\\nHousing constituted the single largest expenditure in both years, expanding substantially from 26% in 2010 to just over a third (34%) of total weekly outlays by 2020. Similarly, spending on utilities and household energy witnessed significant growth, rising from 15% to 20%. Combined, these two essential categories accounted for over half (54%) of household expenditure by the end of the period.\\n\\nIn contrast, allocations for other sectors diminished across the board. Food and groceries, which had accounted for nearly a quarter of expenditure (24%) in 2010, shrank to 20% in 2020. Simultaneously, transport outlays dropped by four percentage points from 18% to 14%, while leisure and entertainment declined from 17% to 12%, representing the smallest budget component by 2020.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Clear overview categorizing essentials vs discretionary costs. Excellent use of proportional phrasing ('just over a third', 'nearly a quarter', 'four percentage points').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear contrast between growing and shrinking sectors\",\n      \"Used fraction/percentage variety ('just over a third')\",\n      \"No arithmetic errors\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"acad-t1-04\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task1_academic\",\n    \"title\": \"Public Transport Satisfaction in Six Global Metropolises\",\n    \"chartType\": \"table\",\n    \"promptText\": \"The table below provides commuter satisfaction ratings (percentage satisfied) across three operational criteria—Punctuality, Cleanliness, and Value for Money—in six major cities in 2023.\\n\\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 150,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 20,\n    \"chartData\": [\n      {\n        \"city\": \"Zurich\",\n        \"Punctuality\": 96,\n        \"Cleanliness\": 92,\n        \"ValueForMoney\": 71\n      },\n      {\n        \"city\": \"Tokyo\",\n        \"Punctuality\": 98,\n        \"Cleanliness\": 95,\n        \"ValueForMoney\": 68\n      },\n      {\n        \"city\": \"Singapore\",\n        \"Punctuality\": 94,\n        \"Cleanliness\": 91,\n        \"ValueForMoney\": 86\n      },\n      {\n        \"city\": \"London\",\n        \"Punctuality\": 78,\n        \"Cleanliness\": 72,\n        \"ValueForMoney\": 49\n      },\n      {\n        \"city\": \"Sydney\",\n        \"Punctuality\": 81,\n        \"Cleanliness\": 76,\n        \"ValueForMoney\": 58\n      },\n      {\n        \"city\": \"New York\",\n        \"Punctuality\": 69,\n        \"Cleanliness\": 54,\n        \"ValueForMoney\": 62\n      }\n    ],\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase\",\n        \"tips\": \"Table comparing customer satisfaction ratings in 6 cities across 3 parameters in 2023.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Overview\",\n        \"focus\": \"Top vs bottom performers\",\n        \"tips\": \"Tokyo, Zurich, and Singapore achieved superior ratings; Value for Money consistently scored lowest.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 1\",\n        \"focus\": \"Top tier (Tokyo, Zurich, Singapore)\",\n        \"tips\": \"Note 90%+ ratings for punctuality and cleanliness, with Singapore leading in value (86%).\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Lower tier (Sydney, London, New York)\",\n        \"tips\": \"Highlight London's low value rating (49%) and New York's bottom scores in cleanliness (54%) and punctuality (69%).\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The table compares public commuter satisfaction levels across three performance indicators—punctuality, cleanliness, and value for money—in six international metropolitan centers during 2023.\\n\\nOverall, Tokyo, Zurich, and Singapore earned decisively superior approval ratings compared to London, Sydney, and New York. Across nearly all surveyed cities, transit users rated punctuality highest, while value for money recorded the lowest satisfaction scores.\\n\\nTokyo and Zurich registered near-flawless performance in operational reliability, with punctuality approval at 98% and 96% respectively. Cleanliness was similarly celebrated in both cities, standing at 95% in Tokyo and 92% in Zurich. However, their value for money was rated substantially lower, at 68% and 71%. Singapore presented the most balanced satisfaction profile, securing over 90% in punctuality and cleanliness, while leading all six cities in perceived affordability at 86%.\\n\\nBy comparison, the remaining three cities scored noticeably lower. Sydney outperformed London and New York with 81% punctuality and 76% cleanliness. London recorded moderate scores for service delivery (78% punctuality, 72% cleanliness), but suffered the poorest value rating at just 49%. Finally, New York commuters reported the lowest satisfaction across the board, with only 69% satisfied with punctuality and a dismal 54% for station and carriage cleanliness.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Superb grouping of top-tier vs lower-tier systems. High precision in reporting data. Varied comparative sentence structures ('outperformed', 'by comparison', 'near-flawless').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Grouped table data logically into two tiers\",\n      \"Noted general trends (punctuality highest, value lowest)\",\n      \"Accurately quoted numbers for all 6 cities\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"acad-t1-05\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task1_academic\",\n    \"title\": \"Industrial Seawater Reverse Osmosis Desalination Process\",\n    \"chartType\": \"process\",\n    \"promptText\": \"The diagram below illustrates the stages involved in producing drinkable freshwater from ocean seawater using modern reverse osmosis desalination technology.\\n\\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 150,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 20,\n    \"chartSvg\": \"<svg viewBox='0 0 600 240' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' class='w-full max-w-xl mx-auto bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900 rounded-xl p-3 border'><rect x='20' y='80' width='80' height='70' rx='8' fill='#0284c7'/><text x='30' y='115' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='white'>1. Ocean Intake</text><text x='35' y='130' font-size='9' fill='#e0f2fe'>Seawater Screen</text><path d='M 100 115 L 140 115' stroke='#0284c7' stroke-width='3' marker-end='url(#arrow)'/><rect x='140' y='80' width='90' height='70' rx='8' fill='#0f766e'/><text x='148' y='115' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='white'>2. Sand Filtration</text><text x='152' y='130' font-size='9' fill='#ccfbf1'>Removes Particles</text><path d='M 230 115 L 270 115' stroke='#0f766e' stroke-width='3'/><rect x='270' y='80' width='100' height='70' rx='8' fill='#0d9488'/><text x='276' y='110' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='white'>3. High-Pressure</text><text x='288' y='125' font-size='9' fill='white'>Pump (70 Bar)</text><path d='M 370 115 L 410 115' stroke='#0d9488' stroke-width='3'/><rect x='410' y='75' width='90' height='80' rx='8' fill='#0369a1'/><text x='418' y='110' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='white'>4. Semi-Permeable</text><text x='422' y='125' font-size='9' fill='#e0f2fe'>Membrane Tube</text><path d='M 500 100 L 540 70' stroke='#10b981' stroke-width='3'/><rect x='520' y='40' width='70' height='45' rx='6' fill='#059669'/><text x='525' y='65' font-size='9' font-weight='bold' fill='white'>5. Freshwater</text><path d='M 500 130 L 540 160' stroke='#e11d48' stroke-width='3'/><rect x='520' y='145' width='70' height='45' rx='6' fill='#be123c'/><text x='525' y='170' font-size='9' font-weight='bold' fill='white'>Brine Discharge</text></svg>\",\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase process\",\n        \"tips\": \"Outline linear man-made process converting raw seawater into potable drinking water.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Overview\",\n        \"focus\": \"Key steps count & dual output\",\n        \"tips\": \"Note 5 principal stages from ocean intake to division into pure freshwater and concentrated brine.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 1\",\n        \"focus\": \"Pre-treatment stages (Intake to Pumping)\",\n        \"tips\": \"Describe raw seawater intake, mechanical screen filtering, and pressurization to 70 bar.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Separation & outputs\",\n        \"tips\": \"Explain passage through semi-permeable membranes, yielding fresh water for distribution and waste brine returning to sea.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The diagram illustrates the sequential stages of the industrial reverse osmosis process used to purify ocean seawater into potable drinking water.\\n\\nOverall, the desalination process comprises five distinct stages, transitioning from initial mechanical filtration through chemical separation, ultimately yielding two distinct outputs: purified freshwater for municipal consumption and hyper-saline brine returned to the sea.\\n\\nIn the preliminary stage, raw seawater is drawn from the ocean through intake pipelines fitted with coarse mesh screens to block large debris and aquatic life. The water then undergoes secondary sand and cartridge filtration, which eliminates finer suspended particulate matter and sediments. Following pre-treatment, the clarified water is directed into a heavy-duty industrial pump, which pressurizes the fluid to approximately 70 bar.\\n\\nNext, the pressurized water is forced into cylindrical reverse osmosis units containing specialized semi-permeable membranes. Under extreme pressure, water molecules penetrate the microscopic pores of the membrane, while dissolved salt ions and organic compounds are rejected. Consequently, the stream separates into two channels: clean freshwater emerges from the upper outlet, where it undergoes mineral remineralization before distribution, while concentrated brine waste is expelled through the lower outlet back into deep oceanic currents.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Superb passive voice usage appropriate for process writing ('is drawn', 'is directed', 'is forced', 'are rejected'). Flawless temporal sequencing ('In the preliminary stage', 'Following pre-treatment', 'Next').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Used passive voice consistently\",\n      \"Clear overview identifying start, end, and two outputs\",\n      \"Sequencing markers used naturally\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"acad-t1-06\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task1_academic\",\n    \"title\": \"Redevelopment of Riverdale Waterfront (2005 vs Present)\",\n    \"chartType\": \"map\",\n    \"promptText\": \"The two maps below illustrate the urban regeneration of the Riverdale industrial docklands between 2005 and the present day.\\n\\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 150,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 20,\n    \"chartSvg\": \"<svg viewBox='0 0 600 240' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' class='w-full max-w-xl mx-auto bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900 rounded-xl p-3 border'><rect x='20' y='20' width='260' height='200' rx='8' fill='#f1f5f9' stroke='#94a3b8'/><text x='100' y='40' font-size='12' font-weight='bold' fill='#475569'>Riverdale 2005</text><rect x='40' y='60' width='90' height='50' fill='#94a3b8'/><text x='50' y='88' font-size='10' fill='white'>Warehouses</text><rect x='150' y='60' width='100' height='50' fill='#64748b'/><text x='165' y='88' font-size='10' fill='white'>Container Yard</text><path d='M 40 160 L 250 160' stroke='#0284c7' stroke-width='24'/><text x='110' y='165' font-size='11' font-weight='bold' fill='white'>Riverdale River</text><rect x='320' y='20' width='260' height='200' rx='8' fill='#f0fdf4' stroke='#86efac'/><text x='390' y='40' font-size='12' font-weight='bold' fill='#15803d'>Riverdale Present Day</text><rect x='340' y='60' width='90' height='50' fill='#059669' rx='4'/><text x='355' y='88' font-size='10' fill='white'>Public Park</text><rect x='450' y='60' width='100' height='50' fill='#0284c7' rx='4'/><text x='460' y='88' font-size='10' fill='white'>Luxury Flats</text><path d='M 340 160 L 550 160' stroke='#0284c7' stroke-width='24'/><text x='410' y='165' font-size='11' font-weight='bold' fill='white'>Marina Promenade</text><circle cx='445' cy='160' r='12' fill='#f59e0b'/><text x='440' y='164' font-size='8' font-weight='bold' fill='white'>Cafes</text></svg>\",\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase map comparison\",\n        \"tips\": \"Identify transformation from industrial dockland to modern commercial and residential area.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Overview\",\n        \"focus\": \"Shift in land use\",\n        \"tips\": \"Heavy industrial infrastructure replaced by public green spaces, residential housing, and leisure.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 1\",\n        \"focus\": \"Northern zone changes\",\n        \"tips\": \"Old warehouses demolished and converted to a public park; container yards replaced by luxury apartment blocks.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Waterfront riverfront transformation\",\n        \"tips\": \"Riverfront converted from working dockland into pedestrian marina promenade with boutique cafes.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The two maps depict the architectural and infrastructural transformation of the Riverdale waterfront area from an industrial zone in 2005 to a revitalized modern precinct at the present time.\\n\\nOverall, the entire district has undergone complete modernization, shifting from a commercial shipping and storage hub into an upscale residential, recreational, and dining destination.\\n\\nIn 2005, the northern territory of the site was occupied predominantly by utilitarian shipping facilities, including derelict brick warehouses in the northwest and an extensive asphalt container storage yard in the northeast. Over the intervening years, these industrial structures were completely demolished. In their place, the western quadrant has been repurposed into a verdant public park with recreational trees, while the eastern quadrant now accommodates a cluster of multi-story luxury residential apartment blocks.\\n\\nSignificant developments also occurred along the riverfront to the south. The original working dockland bordering Riverdale River has been transformed into a landscaped pedestrian promenade with private marina moorings. Furthermore, a new social node comprising outdoor cafes and dining facilities was erected at the center of the boardwalk, providing open-air public amenities where industrial transport once predominated.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Flawless directional language ('northwest', 'eastern quadrant', 'riverfront to the south'). Accurate terminology for redevelopment ('demolished', 'repurposed into', 'modernization').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Accurate directional compass prepositions\",\n      \"Contrasted past industrial vs present residential/leisure\",\n      \"Highlighted all prominent features\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"acad-t1-07\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task1_academic\",\n    \"title\": \"Monthly Average Temperatures and Rainfall in Three Climates\",\n    \"chartType\": \"line\",\n    \"promptText\": \"The line charts below show the monthly average precipitation (in mm) and temperature (in °C) across three global climatic regions over a calendar year.\\n\\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 150,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 20,\n    \"chartData\": [\n      {\n        \"month\": \"Jan\",\n        \"Tropical\": 280,\n        \"Temperate\": 75,\n        \"Arid\": 10\n      },\n      {\n        \"month\": \"Apr\",\n        \"Tropical\": 210,\n        \"Temperate\": 60,\n        \"Arid\": 5\n      },\n      {\n        \"month\": \"Jul\",\n        \"Tropical\": 40,\n        \"Temperate\": 45,\n        \"Arid\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"month\": \"Oct\",\n        \"Tropical\": 190,\n        \"Temperate\": 90,\n        \"Arid\": 8\n      },\n      {\n        \"month\": \"Dec\",\n        \"Tropical\": 310,\n        \"Temperate\": 85,\n        \"Arid\": 12\n      }\n    ],\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase climatic charts\",\n        \"tips\": \"Identify three climate zones, 12 months, and precipitation and temperature metrics.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Overview\",\n        \"focus\": \"Distinctive patterns\",\n        \"tips\": \"Tropical zone experiences monsoon peaks; temperate shows balanced rainfall; arid remains near zero.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 1\",\n        \"focus\": \"Tropical zone dynamics\",\n        \"tips\": \"Highlight intense wet season (Dec-Jan >300mm) and sharp dry dip in July (40mm).\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Temperate vs Arid zones\",\n        \"tips\": \"Contrast temperate consistency (45-90mm) with extreme desert drought (<12mm year-round).\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The graph compares seasonal precipitation patterns across three distinct climatic zones—Tropical, Temperate, and Arid—over a typical twelve-month calendar year, measured in millimeters.\\n\\nOverall, rainfall varies dramatically by ecosystem. The tropical zone displays extreme seasonal volatility characterized by torrential monsoon peaks and a brief dry spell, whereas the temperate region exhibits stable rainfall year-round and the arid zone remains persistently parched.\\n\\nPrecipitation in the tropical climate peaked during the winter months, registering 280 mm in January before surging to a global high of 310 mm in December. However, this region experienced a pronounced dry window in mid-year, with precipitation dropping abruptly to a trough of 40 mm in July.\\n\\nBy contrast, the temperate zone maintained moderate, consistent rainfall throughout all seasons, fluctuating gently between a minimum of 45 mm in July and an autumn peak of 90 mm in October. The arid region recorded negligible rainfall throughout the entire year, never exceeding 12 mm in December and drying up completely to zero precipitation during July.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Precise vocabulary for peaks, troughs, and volatility. Clear paragraph division contrasting seasonal swings vs stability.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear contrast between seasons\",\n      \"Reported peak values and lowest troughs\",\n      \"Grouped similar patterns together\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"acad-t1-08\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task1_academic\",\n    \"title\": \"University Enrollment by Academic Discipline and Gender\",\n    \"chartType\": \"bar\",\n    \"promptText\": \"The bar chart below illustrates the proportion of male and female students enrolled in five university faculties in the United Kingdom in 2023.\\n\\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 150,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 20,\n    \"chartData\": [\n      {\n        \"faculty\": \"Engineering\",\n        \"Male\": 78,\n        \"Female\": 22\n      },\n      {\n        \"faculty\": \"Computer Science\",\n        \"Male\": 72,\n        \"Female\": 28\n      },\n      {\n        \"faculty\": \"Business & Law\",\n        \"Male\": 51,\n        \"Female\": 49\n      },\n      {\n        \"faculty\": \"Humanities\",\n        \"Male\": 38,\n        \"Female\": 62\n      },\n      {\n        \"faculty\": \"Health Sciences\",\n        \"Male\": 25,\n        \"Female\": 75\n      }\n    ],\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase\",\n        \"tips\": \"Bar chart comparing male and female undergraduate ratios across 5 disciplines in the UK (2023).\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Overview\",\n        \"focus\": \"Gender division trends\",\n        \"tips\": \"STEM fields dominated by males; Health and Humanities dominated by females; Business balanced.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 1\",\n        \"focus\": \"Male-dominated disciplines\",\n        \"tips\": \"Engineering (78% vs 22%) and Computer Science (72% vs 28%).\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Female-dominated and parity fields\",\n        \"tips\": \"Health Sciences (75% female), Humanities (62% female), and near 50-50 split in Business & Law.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The bar chart illustrates the gender distribution of university students across five academic disciplines in the United Kingdom during the 2023 academic year, expressed in percentage shares.\\n\\nOverall, the data exhibits distinct gender disparities along disciplinary lines. Male students heavily dominated technical STEM subjects, whereas female students comprised the substantial majority in health and humanities faculties. Business and Law stood out as the sole faculty achieving near-perfect gender parity.\\n\\nIn Engineering and Computer Science, male enrollment outstripped female representation by wide margins. Men accounted for nearly four-fifths (78%) of Engineering cohorts, leaving women with just 22%. A comparable imbalance existed in Computer Science, where male students represented 72% compared to 28% female.\\n\\nConversely, female scholars dominated Health Sciences, constituting three-quarters (75%) of total enrollment, precisely three times the male proportion of 25%. A similar female majority was evident in Humanities at 62%, compared to 38% male. Finally, Business and Law achieved virtually equal balance, with male and female enrollment standing at 51% and 49% respectively.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Superb use of ratio and fraction paraphrasing ('nearly four-fifths', 'three-quarters', 'precisely three times'). Excellent coherence.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Reported proportions accurately\",\n      \"Highlighted Business as parity outlier\",\n      \"No speculative causes introduced\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"acad-t1-09\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task1_academic\",\n    \"title\": \"Biological Life Cycle and Honey Production of Apis Mellifera\",\n    \"chartType\": \"process\",\n    \"promptText\": \"The diagram below illustrates the developmental life cycle of the honeybee and the concurrent steps involved in hive honey production.\\n\\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 150,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 20,\n    \"chartSvg\": \"<svg viewBox='0 0 600 240' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' class='w-full max-w-xl mx-auto bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900 rounded-xl p-3 border'><circle cx='80' cy='120' r='40' fill='#fef3c7' stroke='#d97706' stroke-width='2'/><text x='58' y='124' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='#b45309'>1. Egg (3d)</text><path d='M 125 120 L 165 120' stroke='#d97706' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='210' cy='120' r='40' fill='#fef3c7' stroke='#d97706' stroke-width='2'/><text x='185' y='124' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='#b45309'>2. Larva (6d)</text><path d='M 255 120 L 295 120' stroke='#d97706' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='340' cy='120' r='40' fill='#fef3c7' stroke='#d97706' stroke-width='2'/><text x='315' y='124' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='#b45309'>3. Pupa (12d)</text><path d='M 385 120 L 425 120' stroke='#d97706' stroke-width='3'/><rect x='430' y='80' width='140' height='80' rx='8' fill='#d97706'/><text x='445' y='115' font-size='11' font-weight='bold' fill='white'>4. Adult Forager</text><text x='442' y='135' font-size='9' fill='#fef3c7'>Nectar Gathering & Wax Cell</text></svg>\",\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase natural life cycle\",\n        \"tips\": \"Explain biological maturation stages of the worker honeybee from egg to adult forager.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Overview\",\n        \"focus\": \"Total development time & role\",\n        \"tips\": \"Note 21-day maturation timeline through 4 distinct stages, ending in hive honey production.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 1\",\n        \"focus\": \"Egg and larval stages\",\n        \"tips\": \"Queen lays eggs in wax comb, hatching into larvae after 3 days; fed for 6 days.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Pupation and adult forager tasks\",\n        \"tips\": \"Cell capped for 12 days of pupation; emergence as adult forager collecting nectar and storing honey.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The diagram delineates the developmental life cycle of the worker honeybee (Apis mellifera) from oviposition through to adult foraging and honey production.\\n\\nOverall, the biological cycle spans approximately 21 days across four principal morphological phases: egg, larva, pupa, and mature adult, which ultimately culminates in foraging and nectar transformation inside the hive.\\n\\nThe process initiates when the queen bee deposits a single microscopic egg into a hexagonal wax honeycomb cell. The egg incubates for approximately three days before hatching into a legless larva. Over the subsequent six days, nurse bees feed the rapidly expanding larva, which molts multiple times as its body mass multiplies.\\n\\nAt the end of the larval stage, worker bees seal the cell with a beeswax cap, initiating the pupal stage. During this twelve-day metamorphosis, the organism develops wings, compound eyes, and legs. On day twenty-one, the fully formed adult worker bites through the wax capping. In the final phase of its lifespan, the adult emerges as a forager, harvesting floral nectar and pollen, which is evaporated through wing fanning and stored in wax cells as mature honey.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Precise biological lexicon ('oviposition', 'metamorphosis', 'hexagonal wax honeycomb cell'). Excellent chronological flow.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Accurate timeline sequence reported (3d -> 6d -> 12d = 21d)\",\n      \"Used appropriate passive voice\",\n      \"No external unstated biological assumptions\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"acad-t1-10\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task1_academic\",\n    \"title\": \"Global Coffee Production and Average Export Price (2014–2024)\",\n    \"chartType\": \"bar\",\n    \"promptText\": \"The combination chart below shows total global green coffee production (in millions of 60kg bags) and the average international export price (in USD per kilogram) from 2014 to 2024.\\n\\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 150,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 20,\n    \"chartData\": [\n      {\n        \"year\": \"2014\",\n        \"ProductionBags\": 150,\n        \"ExportPriceUSD\": 3.8\n      },\n      {\n        \"year\": \"2016\",\n        \"ProductionBags\": 158,\n        \"ExportPriceUSD\": 3.2\n      },\n      {\n        \"year\": \"2018\",\n        \"ProductionBags\": 172,\n        \"ExportPriceUSD\": 2.6\n      },\n      {\n        \"year\": \"2020\",\n        \"ProductionBags\": 165,\n        \"ExportPriceUSD\": 3.1\n      },\n      {\n        \"year\": \"2022\",\n        \"ProductionBags\": 168,\n        \"ExportPriceUSD\": 4.6\n      },\n      {\n        \"year\": \"2024\",\n        \"ProductionBags\": 178,\n        \"ExportPriceUSD\": 5.4\n      }\n    ],\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase combination chart\",\n        \"tips\": \"Identify dual metrics: output in millions of 60kg bags and market price in USD per kg over 10 years.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Overview\",\n        \"focus\": \"Inverse vs synchronized periods\",\n        \"tips\": \"Production showed net growth; prices fell initially to a low in 2018 before rallying to record highs by 2024.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 1\",\n        \"focus\": \"Production trend (Bags)\",\n        \"tips\": \"Rise from 150m to peak of 178m, with brief contraction in 2020.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Price dynamics (USD/kg)\",\n        \"tips\": \"Drop from $3.80 to $2.60 in 2018, followed by sharp doubling to $5.40 in 2024.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The combination chart illustrates global green coffee production, measured in millions of 60-kilogram bags, alongside the average international export price per kilogram in US dollars between 2014 and 2024.\\n\\nOverall, coffee production experienced a general upward trend over the decade. Export prices, conversely, followed a pronounced V-shaped trajectory, sliding to a decade low in 2018 before rebounding aggressively to surpass initial levels by 2024.\\n\\nIn terms of harvest output, worldwide production stood at 150 million bags in 2014 and expanded progressively to reach 172 million bags by 2018. Despite a minor contraction to 165 million bags in 2020, production resumed its upward momentum thereafter, climbing to 168 million in 2022 and culminating at a survey peak of 178 million bags in 2024.\\n\\nIn contrast, market prices exhibited substantial volatility. Starting at $3.80 per kilogram in 2014, average prices declined steadily over four years, reaching a trough of $2.60 in 2018 amidst surging global supply. However, this downward trend reversed dramatically over the following six years: prices rebounded to $3.10 in 2020, accelerated to $4.60 in 2022, and reached an all-time high of $5.40 per kilogram by 2024.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Superb handling of dual-axis data. Economic vocabulary ('volatility', 'trough', 'rebounded aggressively', 'decade low').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Reported both metrics with respective units\",\n      \"Identified inverse supply-price relationship in 2018\",\n      \"No confusion between bag counts and dollar prices\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"acad-t1-11\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task1_academic\",\n    \"title\": \"Global Hydropower Production by Region (2010–2024)\",\n    \"chartType\": \"line\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n    \"topic\": \"energy\",\n    \"estimatedBand\": 7.5,\n    \"promptText\": \"The line graph below illustrates hydroelectricity output (in Terawatt-hours) across four regions between 2010 and 2024.\\n\\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 150,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 20,\n    \"chartData\": [\n      {\n        \"year\": \"2010\",\n        \"AsiaPacific\": 720,\n        \"Europe\": 580,\n        \"NorthAmerica\": 640,\n        \"SouthAmerica\": 510\n      },\n      {\n        \"year\": \"2015\",\n        \"AsiaPacific\": 1100,\n        \"Europe\": 600,\n        \"NorthAmerica\": 650,\n        \"SouthAmerica\": 540\n      },\n      {\n        \"year\": \"2020\",\n        \"AsiaPacific\": 1450,\n        \"Europe\": 620,\n        \"NorthAmerica\": 660,\n        \"SouthAmerica\": 570\n      },\n      {\n        \"year\": \"2024\",\n        \"AsiaPacific\": 1800,\n        \"Europe\": 630,\n        \"NorthAmerica\": 670,\n        \"SouthAmerica\": 610\n      }\n    ],\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase prompt\",\n        \"tips\": \"Mention four regions, Terawatt-hour metric, and 14-year timeframe.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Overview\",\n        \"focus\": \"Main regional trend\",\n        \"tips\": \"Asia-Pacific surged dramatically while Western regions exhibited modest, plateaued growth.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 1\",\n        \"focus\": \"Asia-Pacific exponential trajectory\",\n        \"tips\": \"Cite initial 720 TWh rising to 1800 TWh by 2024.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body Paragraph 2\",\n        \"focus\": \"Other three regions\",\n        \"tips\": \"Group Europe, North America, and South America with slight increases.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The line graph delineates the volume of hydroelectricity produced across four continental regions—Asia-Pacific, Europe, North America, and South America—measured in Terawatt-hours (TWh) over a fourteen-year period from 2010 to 2024.\\n\\nOverall, it is manifest that while hydropower generation trended upward across all surveyed territories, the Asia-Pacific region witnessed an extraordinarily steep escalation, solidifying an overwhelming global dominance. In stark contrast, output across North America, Europe, and South America remained comparatively steady throughout the timeframe.\\n\\nIn 2010, the Asia-Pacific led moderately with 720 TWh, followed closely by North America at 640 TWh, Europe at 580 TWh, and South America at 510 TWh. Over the subsequent fourteen years, Asia-Pacific production more than doubled, climbing rapidly to 1100 TWh in 2015 before culminating at an impressive 1800 TWh by 2024.\\n\\nConversely, the remaining three continents followed gentle, plateaued paths. North American generation registered marginal increments, rising slowly from 640 TWh to 670 TWh. Europe and South America followed almost parallel trajectories, ending at 630 TWh and 610 TWh respectively by the conclusion of the survey period.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Task Achievement: Reports key features accurately with precise numerical data. Coherence: Seamless structural transitions ('Overall', 'In stark contrast', 'Conversely'). Lexical Resource: Varied vocabulary ('manifest', 'escalation', 'plateaued paths'). Grammar: Flawless complex sentence forms.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear overview included\",\n      \"Precise data citations with units\",\n      \"Direct comparisons made\",\n      \"Over 150 words\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"acad-t1-12\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task1_ac